  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"115\" data-end=\"248\">The <strong data-start=\"119\" data-end=\"147\">Chromium (Cr) blood test</strong> measures the level of chromium in the bloodstream. Chromium is a trace mineral that plays a role in:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"250\" data-end=\"427\">\n<li data-start=\"250\" data-end=\"315\">\n<p data-start=\"252\" data-end=\"315\"><strong data-start=\"252\" data-end=\"289\">Carbohydrate and lipid metabolism</strong> (especially chromium III)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"316\" data-end=\"346\">\n<p data-start=\"318\" data-end=\"346\"><strong data-start=\"318\" data-end=\"346\">Enhancing insulin action</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"347\" data-end=\"427\">\n<p data-start=\"349\" data-end=\"427\"><strong data-start=\"349\" data-end=\"402\">Monitoring occupational or environmental exposure</strong> to <strong data-start=\"406\" data-end=\"427\">toxic chromium VI</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"429\" data-end=\"460\">This test is primarily used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"462\" data-end=\"689\">\n<li data-start=\"462\" data-end=\"577\">\n<p data-start=\"464\" data-end=\"577\">Detect <strong data-start=\"471\" data-end=\"492\">chromium toxicity</strong> (especially from industrial exposure or implants like metal-on-metal hip prostheses)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"578\" data-end=\"635\">\n<p data-start=\"580\" data-end=\"635\">Monitor <strong data-start=\"588\" data-end=\"619\">nutritional supplementation</strong> or deficiencies</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"636\" data-end=\"689\">\n<p data-start=\"638\" data-end=\"689\">Investigate symptoms related to <strong data-start=\"670\" data-end=\"689\">metal poisoning</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong data-start=\"670\" data-end=\"689\">Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition / Cause</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Industrial exposure to chromium VI</strong></td>\n<td>Found in welding, chrome plating, dyes &ndash; toxic and carcinogenic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Metal implant corrosion</strong></td>\n<td>Wear and tear from orthopedic implants can elevate chromium levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Excessive supplementation</strong></td>\n<td>Overuse of chromium-containing supplements may cause toxicity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Renal dysfunction</strong></td>\n<td>May reduce chromium excretion, leading to accumulation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Occupational exposure</strong></td>\n<td>Prolonged skin or inhalation contact can increase systemic levels</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
